  "description": "Construct a bounded token vocabulary sorted by frequency with <UNK> fallback and map text into integer sequences.\n\nLanguage vocabulary exhibits heavy-tailed Zipfian frequencies; pruning rare words to <UNK> prevents parameter explosion.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nV = \\{ \\text{<UNK>} \\} \\cup \\operatorname{TopK}_{w}(\\operatorname{Freq}(w))\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Count word occurrences across training corpus\n2. Rank tokens by frequency with alphabetical tie-breaking\n3. Assign integer IDs to top vocabulary words with UNK fallback mapping\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'vocabulary' list and 'encoded_corpus' sequence IDs.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Vocabulary Building\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **Vocabulary Building** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nLanguage vocabulary exhibits heavy-tailed Zipfian frequencies; pruning rare words to <UNK> prevents parameter explosion. In this problem, the goal is to: construct a bounded token vocabulary sorted by frequency with <unk> fallback and map text into integer sequences.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nV = \\{ \\text{<UNK>} \\} \\cup \\operatorname{TopK}_{w}(\\operatorname{Freq}(w))\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Count word occurrences across training corpus\n2. **Phase 2**: Rank tokens by frequency with alphabetical tie-breaking\n3. **Phase 3**: Assign integer IDs to top vocabulary words with UNK fallback mapping\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: corpus = [\"the cat sat on the mat\", \"the dog sat on the rug\"], max_vocab_size = 4, unk_token = \"<UNK>\"\n\nOutput: {\"vocabulary\": [\"<UNK>\", \"the\", \"on\", \"sat\"], \"encoded_corpus\": [[1, 0, 3, 2, 1, 0], [1, 0, 3, 2, 1, 0]]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated nlp_vocabulary_building under standard test conditions."
